WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.640
Back to the Future's Christopher Lloyd went
from being an A-lister at the top of his game

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.740
to pretty much disappearing entirely.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.120
For those of you who've been missing him,
don't worry!

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.980
He wasn't dissolved in dip or sent back in
time to the old west.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.360
Here's what he's been up to lately.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.820
Struggling actors will typically take whatever
parts are offered to them in major studio

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.320
films.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.780
Then, once they've had a run of successful
projects, they'll often start getting pickier.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.840
This has been the reason that many famous
actors have suddenly disappeared soon after

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.680
hitting it big.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.240
They're no longer desperate, so they only
work when a role truly speaks to them.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.760
But strangely enough, for Christopher Lloyd,
just the opposite is true.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.930
If you check out his filmography, you'll see
that he's still working constantly, and the

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.220
reason we aren't seeing him popping up in
major films is that he isn't picky at all.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.150
He has stated on multiple occasions that he
doesn't mind small roles in small projects.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.940
For instance, in an interview with Metro New
York, he said,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.200
"Unless it's total garbage, I'll work, whatever
comes along.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.960
The money might not be fabulous.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:00:59.940
But I don't mind that."

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.900
Even though he hasn't been a recurring cast
member on a big TV show since his days on

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.860
Taxi, Lloyd still spends plenty of time on
the small screen, albeit usually in brief

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:10.980
guest appearances.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.460
For instance, he appeared on the Roseanne
revival as Bev's comically elderly boyfriend

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:15.960
Lou.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.060
He also showed up in The Big Bang Theory as
Theodore, a creepy vagrant who Sheldon rents

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.940
his room out to as part of a revenge plot
against Leonard.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.380
If you go back a little further, Lloyd has
had no shortage of great TV appearances.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.580
He was in the 100th episode of Psych, which
was a loving homage to his film Clue and also

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.060
featured several of his co-stars from that
movie.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:41.140
He was also on an episode of Law &amp; Order:
Criminal Intent, because apparently all actors

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.630
are required to appear on at least one Law
&amp; Order show at some point in their career.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.470
And of course we can't forget his regular
gig on the short-lived sitcom Stacked alongside

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.480
Pamela Anderson.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.620
"Was I wearing a belt when I came in?"

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.540
Lloyd has also been spending plenty of his
time lately on social media.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.560
His platform of choice is Twitter, where he
tweets under the handle @DocBrownLloyd.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.520
His feed largely consists of chatting with
fans, retweeting Back the Future memes, and

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.180
speaking his mind about our current political
climate.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.220
Lloyd's feed is certainly an entertaining
place to be, but let's hope he can remember

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.830
to use the internet responsibly.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.360
We still want to see him on the big and medium
screens, rather than spending all his time

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.840
on the smallest screen of all.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.090
Over the past few years, Lloyd has spent a
decent chunk of his free time travelling around

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.340
the country hosting live screenings of his
films, followed by Q&amp;A sessions.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.880
He's also no stranger to the convention circuit,
including a 2016 Wizard World Philadelphia

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.620
appearance alongside his Back to the Future
co-stars Lea Thompson and Michael J Fox.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.040
Lloyd has never shied away from appearing
publicly or interacting with fans.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.120
While at Seattle's Emerald City Comic Con
in 2018, he spoke about how gratifying it

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.750
was to hear how fans have been affected by
his work.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:02.040
"Back to the Future changed their lives, or
they chose certain careers because of the

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.520
influence of the movies."

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.060
Keep an eye on your local convention circuit!

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.710
Even if you're not seeing Christopher Lloyd
much in movie theaters anymore, there are

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.980
more chances than ever to talk to him in person.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.960
One unfortunate reason that we're not seeing
more of Christopher Lloyd is simply because

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.060
Back to the Future is probably never coming
back.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.760
Lloyd himself isn't the problem, as he still
loves playing Doc Brown in cameo appearances

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.140
and other small occasions.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.500
"Are the Cubs in the playoff?"

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.040
“Yeah, they actually are!”

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.440
But the producers are definitively against
it, though, and there's also the matter of

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.420
Michael J Fox's health issues.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.860
Fox has been battling Parkinson's disease
publicly since 1998, and though he's acted

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.310
regularly since then, a leading part in a
new Back to the Future may be more than he

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.400
could comfortably handle.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:55.000
When asked about the prospect of a sequel,
director Robert Zemeckis told The Telegraph,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.140
"Oh, God no … I mean, to me, that’s outrageous.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.880
Especially since it’s a good movie.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.920
It’s like saying ‘Let’s remake Citizen
Kane.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.680
Who are we going to get to play Kane?’

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.820
What folly, what insanity is that?

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.440
Why would anyone do that?”

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.960
The truth is that Christopher Lloyd was always
difficult to cast.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.960
He has a unique energy that is perfect for
some very specific roles.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.660
So perhaps he was always destined to be a
mid-level character actor.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.820
He used to make up for the limited demand
for eccentric weirdos in big films by playing

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.630
a very wide age range, bouncing back and forth
between older and younger characters from

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:29.380
film to film.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.940
Now that Lloyd's becoming an old man for real,
his options are getting more limited.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:38.640
In an interview with Metro New York, he hinted
at the difficulties that have come with getting

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.260
older, saying,

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:44.260
"I once did a Q&amp;A for Back to the Future,
and some little kid asks for the microphone

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.760
and says, 'Mr. Lloyd, now that you're older
do you miss putting on old age makeup?'"

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.000
Lloyd chuckled heartily at that precocious
question, which hopefully means he's made

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.060
peace with getting up there in years.

